 ## Overview
-The Single Scattering (Blinn Model) is a light transport approximation for participating media that accounts for exactly one secondary scattering event between the observer and a light source [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of rendering volumetric effects like clouds by assuming low albedo, thereby ignoring multiple scattering interactions [S2, unofficial]. It builds on the general Single Scattering Model by applying Blinn's 1982 approximations for uniform, low-density media [S1, unofficial].
